Related, not a source of copied files: the Neovim ecosystem also has a
well-known Tokyo Night port, folke/tokyonight.nvim
(https://github.com/folke/tokyonight.nvim, Apache License 2.0, commit
cdc07ac78467a233fd62c493de29a17e0cf2b2b6). It shares the same palette
lineage and is credited here for completeness, but Madoe does not copy
files from it and makes no claim that its maintainer (Folke Lemaitre)
personally authored the original accent palette — that credit belongs to
Enkia's tokyo-night-vscode-theme above.

Modifications: only a subset of accent hues was carried into Madoe's Zed
syntax map, Ghostty ANSI palette, VS Code token colors, and T3 Code UI
colors, adapted for
contrast against Madoe's neutral background ramp rather than the original
theme's colored (blue-tinted) backgrounds, while keeping most hues intact.

================================================================================
Summary of changes made by Madoe
================================================================================

- Restructured both palettes into a single, editor-agnostic source of
  truth, palette.json, covering both a dark ("Madoe Night") and light
  ("Madoe Day") variant.
- Generated Ghostty terminal configs, VS Code color-theme JSON, and a T3
  Code theme file from that one source instead of hand-maintaining four
  separate formats.
- Renamed the themes to "Madoe Night" / "Madoe Day" and re-attributed
  authorship to anjaloh, while keeping this notice so the origin of the
  underlying color choices remains traceable.
- Adapted hex values where needed for contrast/consistency across Zed,
  Ghostty, VS Code, and T3 Code, rather than mechanically re-exporting the
  upstream projects' original values as-is.
